description:
Bug found in fresh-eyes review of crates/frankenterm-core/src/incident_bundle.rs (PrivacyBudget — privacy-critical).

## Defect

truncate_file_content at incident_bundle.rs:268-283 reserves 40 bytes for the truncation marker:

    let mut end = self.max_bytes_per_file.saturating_sub(40);
    ...
    let marker = format!(
        "\n... [truncated at {} bytes, limit {}]",
        content.len(),
        self.max_bytes_per_file
    );
    format!("{}{marker}", &content[..end])

But the marker length depends on the digit-count of content.len() AND max_bytes_per_file. The fixed prefix is ~34 chars; with 8-digit content.len() and 9-digit limit, the marker is ~46-51 bytes — **exceeding the 40-byte reservation by 6-11 bytes**.

## Repro

    let budget = PrivacyBudget { max_bytes_per_file: 1000, ... };
    let content = "x".repeat(10_000_000);  // 8-digit length
    let truncated = budget.truncate_file_content(&content);
    assert!(truncated.len() <= 1000);  // FAILS: actual is ~1006

## Why it matters

PrivacyBudget is the load-bearing mechanism for incident-bundle privacy. The contract: every file in a bundle stays within max_bytes_per_file. A bypass here means audit/debug bundles can leak 6-11 bytes of secret content past the documented limit per truncation event. Across many files in a bundle, this compounds.

The bug also affects bundle-size accounting: would_exceed_total uses max_bytes_per_file as its accounting unit; if individual files exceed it, the total can exceed max_total_bytes too.

## Fix

Compute the marker length first, then derive the safe content boundary:

    let marker = format!("\n... [truncated at {} bytes, limit {}]", content.len(), self.max_bytes_per_file);
    let mut end = self.max_bytes_per_file.saturating_sub(marker.len());
    while end > 0 && !content.is_char_boundary(end) {
        end -= 1;
    }
    format!("{}{marker}", &content[..end])

## Acceptance

- truncate_file_content output length always <= max_bytes_per_file.
- Regression test reproduces the bug + asserts the fix.
- Existing tests still pass.

## Cross-references

- crates/frankenterm-core/src/incident_bundle.rs:268-283
- ft-vgtab (sibling — privacy invariant in cold_tier_pipeline)

## Site
`crates/frankenterm-core/src/mcp_framework.rs:69-129` — `OutboundFrameworkClient` struct and impl.

## Defect
The wrapper exposes `connect_stdio`, `list_tool_definitions`, `call_tool_content` but no:
- `shutdown(&mut self) -> Result<()>`
- `disconnect(&mut self) -> Result<()>`
- `close(self)`

Callers cannot deterministically tear down a stdio connection. They rely entirely on the inner `FrameworkClient`'s Drop impl (from fastmcp, external crate) to clean up the spawned subprocess and pipes.

## Concerns
1. **No graceful shutdown**: a busy stdio subprocess might be SIGKILL'd at Drop instead of receiving a polite shutdown notification.
2. **No error visibility on cleanup**: if Drop encounters an error (zombie process, pipe close failure), it swallows the error per Rust convention. Caller has no signal.
3. **No lifecycle invariant for re-use**: if the inner FrameworkClient becomes unusable (subprocess crashed), there's no `is_alive()` or `reconnect()` API. The Mutex<FtMcpClient> in mcp_proxy.rs holds the wrapper indefinitely.

## Compounds with ft-xhj38
ft-xhj38 noted that `Mutex<FtMcpClient>` per-server in compose_proxy_tools head-of-line blocks all proxied calls when remote hangs. Without an explicit disconnect path, recovering from a wedged remote requires dropping the entire builder and rebuilding — there's no "kill this connection and reconnect" API.

## Fix shape
Add minimal lifecycle methods:

```rust
impl OutboundFrameworkClient {
    /// Gracefully terminate the stdio connection. Returns Ok if
    /// the inner Client signals clean shutdown; Err if the
    /// transport reported failure during teardown.
    pub(crate) fn shutdown(self) -> Result<(), FrameworkMcpError> {
        // Defer to fastmcp::Client::shutdown if available, else
        // explicit drop with logging.
        drop(self.inner);
        Ok(())
    }

    /// Test whether the underlying transport is still alive.
    pub(crate) fn is_alive(&self) -> bool {
        // Defer to fastmcp::Client::is_connected if available.
        true  // pessimistic default
    }
}
```

Requires fastmcp API surface inspection; if missing, file upstream.

## Class
P3 lifecycle invariant. Cross-cuts ft-xhj38; pair them in a transport-layer cleanup epic.

